                         "summary":  "An electromechanical machine that helped search for Enigma keys during the Second World War.",
                         "historicalContext":  "British work built on pre-war Polish cryptanalysis. Turing\u0027s approach used guessed plaintext fragments, called cribs, rather than relying on the earlier indicator method.",
                         "howItWorks":  "Operators wired a crib-derived menu. Rotating drums tested candidate settings; possible stops needed checking. Welchman\u0027s diagonal board reduced false candidates.",
                         "impact":  "Bombes accelerated recovery of Enigma keys and helped codebreakers read intercepted military traffic.",
                         "editorialNotes":  [
                                                {
                                                    "kind":  "scope",
                                                    "text":  "A Bombe searched for candidate keys; it was not a general-purpose computer or a machine that simply printed every intercepted message in plaintext.",
